  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Dwarf Cellophane-cuckoo Bee (Epeolus pusillus) is a small parasitic bee belonging to the family Apidae, native to New England and eastern North America. Unlike most bees, it does not collect pollen or nectar; instead, it lays eggs in the nests of sweat bees (Halictus species), where its larvae feed on the host's provisions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Tiny Size",
      "description": "At only 4-6 mm long, this is one of the smallest bee species, making it easily overlooked.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Hairless Abdomen",
      "description": "The shiny, relatively hairless abdomen with pale bands is characteristic of cuckoo bees and distinguishes it from fuzzy native bees.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Black and White Markings",
      "description": "Features a predominantly black body with white or cream-colored facial and abdominal bands.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in open sunny areas, gardens, and meadows of New England where its host sweat bees (Halictus species) establish their ground nests.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a cuckoo bee, Epeolus pusillus has evolved to mimic the appearance and chemical signals of its host sweat bees, allowing it to sneak into their nests undetected to parasitize their brood.",
